gork (1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: gork for UEFI, x86-64 and arm64 (gork-<version>-uefi.zip):
    gorkx64.efi and gorkaa64.efi run from the UEFI shell with no operating
    system, in batch mode, on the firmware's TCP/IP, or on a TCP/IP stack
    of gork's own where the firmware has none (U-Boot, or a PC with its
    network stack turned off).
  * New: gork as a barebox command, on arm64
    (gork-<version>-barebox-arm64.zip): a barebox with `gork` built in,
    on gork's own TCP/IP.
  * New: /save and /load in the TUI keep a conversation in
    _gork/session.jsn and bring it back.
  * New: configure --without-tui and --without-mcp, for systems that lack
    curses or fork; --with-max-file-size=BYTES sets the size the file
    tools take (it replaces --with-max-read); and Unicode is on by default
    where ncursesw and locale.h are found.
  * The tool lines show the path of the file a file tool works on.
  * --version names the build: v<version>-beta-<commits> from git, with
    -dirty for changed files, and the version alone for a release.
  * When a task stops at max_turns, the TUI keeps the work it did in the
    conversation instead of dropping it.  The scrollback is capped, and
    the TUI redraws faster.  A retried request waits as long as the
    server's Retry-After asks.  Host names are looked up once, and the
    tool list is built once, not for every request.
  * Windows: the replies of /new and /effort (and the new /save and
    /load) reach the TUI's chat pane; stderr was buffered.
  * Minix scrolls the TUI correctly.
  * The tests run through gork-relay 0.3, and the --without-tui and
    --without-mcp builds go through the suite too.

 -- Yeold <isac.selea@gmail.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:55:08 +0100

gork (1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: packages for FreeBSD 15.1, NetBSD 11.0 and OpenBSD 7.9, on amd64
    and arm64: static binaries (gork-<version>-<os>-<arch>.tar.gz),
    cross-built with clang against each release's own base sets
    (bsdbuild.sh), and each run on its own system in QEMU before release.
    The OpenBSD ones are for 7.9 only.
  * New: Plan 9 (9front, through APE), in batch mode: there is no curses,
    so notui.c stands in for the TUI.
  * Newly confirmed, built and run in emulators: AIX PS/2 1.3 and AIX
    4.3.3, HP-UX 10.20 and 11i v1, OS/2 Warp 3 Connect and 4.52, Minix
    2.0.4, NeXTSTEP 3.3 and OPENSTEP 4.2, SunOS 4.1.4, System V Release
    4.0 (Dell Unix), BeOS R5, QNX 6.2.1, Mac OS X 10.4 on PowerPC, and
    Debian and NetBSD on m68k.  What they need is in docs/build/, one
    guide for each system built by hand.
  * The TUI draws dim text plainly on QNX's qansi terminal, where dim is
    black.
  * testing/: fakeapi.c, a fake Messages API in C89 for systems without
    Python; vmcheck.sh, the batch checks in plain sh; mcp.sh, an MCP
    server in sh; and bsdtest.py, which runs the BSD packages in QEMU.

 -- Yeold <isac.selea@gmail.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:20:00 +0200

gork (1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: streamed replies.  With `stream` (auto: on in the TUI, off in
    batch mode) the text shows as it arrives, for both protocols.  A
    chunked reply is decoded.
  * New: the TUI has a line editor (Left, Right, Home, End, Delete, ^A,
    ^E), Up and Down through the lines sent before, and input of
    several lines (a backslash, then Enter).  Esc also stops an MCP call.
  * New: MCP servers over streamable HTTP: an entry with a "url" instead
    of a "command", with "headers" if it wants a token.  gork has no TLS:
    an https:// server is reached through a "relay" that has.  Entries in
    Zed's layout, with a "transport", are read too.
  * New: docs/examples has sample .gork.conf, gork.cnf and mcp.json.
  * DOS: both builds are the bare bones: no streaming, no input of
    several lines, and no MCP.  The 16-bit build gets its address by
    DHCP if asked.
  * Newly confirmed: MS-DOS 2.11 (the 16-bit build), DR-DOS 7.03 (both),
    Windows 3.0 and 3.1 in standard mode (the 16-bit build), MS-DOS 6.22
    with HIMEM and EMM386, and SPX through Novell's IPXODI and through a
    WSGEN-made IPX.COM.  The 16-bit build on an emulated 8086 and 286.
  * DOS, 32-bit: the time zone is set up first.  A stat() before the
    first time() leaves DJGPP's clock stuck, so that a request can be
    neither waited out nor aborted; no release had one, but the next
    change might have.
  * Fixed: DOS, 16-bit: a task in the TUI without a packet driver loaded
    ended gork with the screen as curses left it.
  * Fixed: DOS, 16-bit: on MS-DOS 2 gork found no gork.cnf, and the TUI
    hung waiting for a key.
  * Fixed: Windows: gork.exe held two instructions a 486 lacks, from
    MinGW's start-up code.

 -- Yeold <isac.selea@gmail.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:46 +0200

gork (1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The first full release.  gork is free software under the GNU GPL,
    version 3 or later.
  * New: a 16-bit DOS build, gork-1.0-dos16.zip: real mode, 640K,
    for the 8086 and up, and for where no protected mode is to be had
    (Windows/386 2.x).  A conversation is kept small there.
  * New: SPX.  On an IPX network without TCP/IP, the DOS and Windows
    builds reach a relay with `relay = spx:NET.NODE:SOCKET`.  The relay
    is gork-relay, a program of its own (github.com/yeold/gork-relay),
    which also does what the socat line did.
  * New: static Linux binaries for ten CPUs, gork-1.0-linux-*.tar.gz:
    amd64, i386, arm64, armhf, riscv64, s390x, ppc64el, powerpc, m68k
    and hppa.
  * Windows: gork-1.0-win32.zip is now the Cygwin build, packed to
    run without Cygwin, for 64-bit Windows 10 and later; it has MCP
    servers.  The native build is gork-1.0-win32-legacy.zip, for
    everything back to Windows 95, and has none.  It gains gorknt3.exe,
    for Windows NT 3.1 and 3.5.
  * Newly confirmed, the DOS build: MS-DOS 3.30 and 4.01, and DOS boxes
    under Windows 1.04, 2.03, 2.11, 3.0 and 3.1.  The Windows build:
    Windows NT 3.1, 3.51, 4.0, 2000 and XP, Windows 95 and Me.  Linux on
    m68k, powerpc and hppa.
  * Fixed: on MS-DOS 4.01 edit_file crashed inside DJGPP's stat().
  * Fixed: a Windows console showed non-ASCII text as its UTF-8 bytes.
  * Fixed: on 32-bit Linux, a directory on a file system with 64-bit
    inode numbers was listed as empty.
  * Fixed: the temporary file of a write has an 8.3 name on Windows too,
    which a FAT disk under Windows NT 3.1 needs.
  * Hardening, after a code scan: paths and the request head are no
    longer built in fixed buffers; ~/.gork/trusted is created readable by
    its owner only.
  * `protocol = openai` is confirmed against a real server.
  * README: a table of the tested systems, the packages, and which
    builds have MCP servers; the developer notes moved to docs/.

 -- Yeold <isac.selea@gmail.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:17:08 +0200

gork (0.10~beta) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The TUI shows the version: in its top bar, after "gork", and in the
    window title, where the terminal has one.
  * The top bar now has the context size before the model, so that a
    narrow terminal cuts off the working directory, not the context.
  * DOS and Windows: messages that pointed at ~/.gork.conf now name the
    file gork really reads there, with its full path.  The README has a
    table of the file and directory names on Unix, DOS and Windows.
  * Windows: gork.exe is confirmed working on Windows 11, TUI included.

